{"kind":"report","text":"Technical decision forb5446a29 under archive inventory Source7e39eb4b, following78127fe6 and approved1815b3dd. Proceed with the local-native, two-phase inventory direction and proposed conservative caps, with the explicit read/work qualifications below. No separate broad prerequisite is required if these native metadata facts can be obtained as specified; otherwise stop at that missing admission prerequisite instead of reading forbidden payloads to infer them. This is advice, not Source approval, certification or export permission.\n\nAdmission/coverage: bind actual native full ScopeRef/incarnation, pinned definition/support policy and exact cutoff seq/hash before selection. Resolve the admitted pin from bounded native metadata/trusted registry, not a caller assertion. Preserve archive-policy's exact membership@6-or-trusted-certified-digest rule; legacy/unadmitted pins refuse before entry/dependency body selection. Native preflight must account for every entry0..through, including genesis and earlier archived portions, before concluding absence of custom domains/foreign copies. Verify contiguity, sequence/size/hash metadata and cutoff correspondence, plus complete supported owner/domain declarations and selected declaration roots. Missing/malformed/ambiguous/unknown metadata or a stopped scan never means false. The resulting evidence is scoped to this pinned selected prefix/cutoff, not a statement about later entries or perpetual future cohort safety.\n\nPrivacy: use actual uses/consumer/owner metadata and exact supported declarations; do not accept request booleans, infer domains from arbitrary strings, or execute untrusted package code to classify them. A custom domain, foreign copy or unsupported owner anywhere in the selected prefix refuses the whole inventory, not just its row/dependency. Do not fetch custom-value bodies, copied foreign-entry bodies or detached-text bodies during classification. Bounded declaration metadata needed to classify an admitted root/closure is permitted, under the same budget. An exact-root certificate is trusted reviewer configuration, not automatic certification of arbitrary unknown declarations/owners reached later; if its policy coverage is insufficient, refuse unsupported. No new certified digest is granted here. Keep room consent/access disclosure/writer admission owed before actual export.\n\nCost of preflight is real work: LIMIT4097 scalar metadata may detect the4096 cap, but a tiny projected result does not bound JSON work inside SQLite. First obtain bounded byte-length/sequence metadata without JSON-extracting entire bodies; validate/reserve cumulative participating entry sizes before JSON extraction/json_each or body parse. Bound the returned metadata shape (counts/booleans/bounded owner names, not unbounded arrays), native SQL calls and scanned rows. Native entryBytes currently256KiB and4096 entries permit1GiB of stored bytes, so row count alone does not satisfy64MiB. Do not SUM/JSON-scan beyond the bounded selected range before applying the work limit. If the implementation cannot do this with existing native rows, a separately reviewed derived metadata/index prerequisite is needed; it must never backfill unbounded history inside an allegedly bounded pass.\n\nInitial limits are an acceptable deliberately limited availability contract: one local scope; at most4096 whole-prefix entries; at most512 retained attempts including failed probes and cache-hit/validation requests; at most64 aggregate unique visited declaration nodes; one cumulative64MiB raw/input-work allowance; one original30s logical pass deadline. Keep native per-reply/entry/definition/depth and per-root namedDefinitions limits as additional ceilings (current native namedDefinitions16; document its existing root-count convention).64 aggregate nodes is not permission to enlarge any one native closure. Charge/reserve metadata inspection, SQL JSON parsing input bytes, raw replies and repeated decode/validation work honestly under declared ledgers: do not call64MiB a whole-work bound if it limits only returned bytes. If using separate raw-transfer and parse/metadata counters, name each explicitly and retain an enforced finite aggregate limit; no reset per entry/root, top-up, uncharged cache path or partial-success manifest.\n\nState additional finite invocation/metadata limits in Source. A conservative initial ceiling of16384 native SQL invocations and4MiB projected metadata across this pass is acceptable, subject to actual witness counts; count helper/size probes as well as body calls. They do not replace the64MiB input-work ceiling. Bound source work before allocating/parsing, and check deadline/active state before/after awaited or synchronous native work. A timed-out pending task must not continue SQL/dependency traversal/cache installation or publish a manifest later; one operation owns its remaining time. No physical preemption or global heap guarantee is inferred. Return explicit unavailable/limit/incomplete, never complete prefix, if any bound stops admission or selection. In particular larger prefixes are unavailable initially: do not describe this as supporting every100000-entry cohort or silently choose an earlier through to make a caller's requested cutoff appear complete.\n\nSharing/layering: keep raw canonical/hash/key helpers in bytes (retainedValueKey already exists); put pure semantic consumer planning/reference grammar in derive, which already owns placesOf/inputTexts/retainsOf/evidenceValues and can depend on contract+bytes. Both verifier and Scope's native scanner can use it without a scope/replay/Git import cycle. Keep native SQL/admission/authority orchestration in scope; Git layout/writer consumes a completed typed inventory, not a runtime import of scope. A mechanically shared creates() extraction with the existing scope re-export is appropriate, but prove identical native behavior and preserve missing-unused-child behavior. Do not turn bytes into a module that imports derive/platform code or copy another variant switch into the scanner.\n\nThe closed consumer/purpose contract must represent local full scope+entry variant/position/hash, exact kind/domain/digest, and required versus ordered probe versus explicit text omission. Choose keys from the native typed declarations, validate bytes before selection, charge failed attempts, deduplicate storage objects while retaining per-entry reasons. Unknown consumer/owner refuses. Share these planning/validation helpers at the verifier's actual read sites (including direct text/#hand), keeping proof/history/tombstone traversal and all ordinary replay outcomes where they belong. A local scan is not verify(). It neither follows copied foreign dependencies nor replays selected definition children as a new ordinary-replay requirement. Preserve snapshot/ancestry/trust qualifications.\n\nSource witnesses must include: full-prefix preflight reaching a forbidden item at the last admitted position with zero forbidden body reads; cutoff/incarnation/hash/size/owner mismatch; exact4096/4097 and byte/call/attempt/node/deadline boundaries; genuinely admitted metadata before parsing versus a huge-row/JSON-work counterexample; legacy rejection before payloads; no stopped-scan privacy claim; required/probe/omission differences; all8 variants and newly unsupported consumer refusal; native definition-key parity; separate archive-only closure with unchanged replay; missing child later becoming available for the same cutoff while unrelated later inputs stay excluded; no late continuation after timeout. Budget witnesses should observe native query/byte admissions, not merely a fake that returns a supplied safe flag. Reuse prior native/fold/service tests at meaningful boundaries and require one frozen gate/exact-head review. No exporter/writer/clone/privacy certificate or provider/push/deploy action is approved by this advice.","body":{"formal_approval":"false","no_git_artifact":"true","promise":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:8a9d30bd302f9ab930242cb676f19bc750100afe","request":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:b5446a29f895eca9b24f7f2c64be70789644489e"}}